Id: lin050w
Severity: WARN
Category: network
Check: check_sysctl
Fix: Set net.ipv4.tcp_timestamps=1 in /etc/sysctl.d.
References: Linux kernel networking documentation (ip-sysctl, tcp_timestamps); RFC 7323
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST CM-6, SC-7; ISO 8.20; CE Secure configuration

net.ipv4.tcp_timestamps is 2: TCP timestamps are sent, and all from
one clock. A host elsewhere can read the clock's progress and tell
how long this one has been up, and recognise its connections as one
host's across addresses and over time. 1, the kernel's default on
every distribution, gives each connection a random offset so the
clock says nothing; 0 turns timestamps off, and with them the
round-trip measurement and the protection against wrapped sequence
numbers that RFC 7323 uses them for. Nothing ships 2: it was set
here, and it undoes the reason 1 exists.
  # sysctl -w net.ipv4.tcp_timestamps=1
